tion, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}-4{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{4x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{4x})}^{′}-4(u{e}^{4x}) & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+4u{e}^{4x}-4u{e}^{4x} & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x}+\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}}-\\cancel{4u{e}^{4x}} & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{4x} & =\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ {u}^{′} & ={e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & ={e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{4x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{4x} \\\\  & =\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x\\cdot {e}^{4x} \\\\  & ={e}^{4x}\\int {e}^{-4x}\\operatorname{cosh}⁡5x\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478407 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478276 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478407 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337239" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478407 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478407 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+5y=(2{x}^{2}-1){e}^{3x}.\n$$\n\nAccording to the method of variation of parameters, which of the following is a suitable ansatz for the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478407 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "605f7436-db66-5e71-900c-b1554cd28ac4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478407 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-5x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478442 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478442 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478288 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478442 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186478295 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478442 #edb/ref 1069 "q-337516" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478442 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478442 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the first-order linear ODE\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+6y={e}^{-6x}.\n$$\n\nWe wish to solve this equation using the method of variation of parameters (VOP).\n\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n$\\,$ According to VOP, we assume a particular solution of the form {{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Solving for $u,$ we have $u(x)=$ {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Therefore, the particular solution is ${y}_{p}(x)=$ {{field-3}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478442 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "bcdda2fe-c778-5147-ad87-b07c607973b4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186478442 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+6{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nThe method of variation of parameters constructs a particular solution by varying the constant in the homogeneous solution. So, to find a suitable ${y}_{p},$ we set $A=u(x)$ in the complementary solution, as given by the following ansatz:\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=u(x){e}^{-6x}\n$$\n\nIntuitively, this form of ${y}_{p}$ tracks the homogeneous solution but with a varying amplitude.\n\nTo find $u,$ we substitute our ansatz into the equation, apply the product rule to the derivative term, and simplify to isolate ${u}^{′}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{(u{e}^{-6x})}^{′}+6(u{e}^{-6x}) & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-6x}-6u{e}^{-6x}+6u{e}^{-6x} & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-6x}-\\cancel{6u{e}^{-6x}}+\\cancel{6u{e}^{-6x}} & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′}{e}^{-6x} & ={e}^{-6x} \\\\ {u}^{′} & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides with respect to $x$ (omitting the arbitrary constant), we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{u}^{′} & =1 \\\\ \\int {u}^{′}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\,\\text{d}x \\\\ u & =x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, since ${y}_{p}=u(x){e}^{-6x},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}_{p} & =u(x){e}^{-6x} \\\\  & =x{e}^{-6x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502983 #edb/ref 1080 "\\int {e}^{-2x}(x+3)\\,\\text{d}x" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502983 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186502983 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "01e6512f-2f3d-5c8c-92fc-e571cc20629